workedExample:
Without an applicable default Trusted Types policy, an ordinary string passed to this enforced innerHTML sink throws TypeError. A policy can produce TrustedHTML, but safety depends on its implementation. With DOMPurify loaded, a fragment is:

```javascript
// Sanitise before creating TrustedHTML.
const p = trustedTypes.createPolicy(
  "app", {createHTML: s =>
    DOMPurify.sanitize(s, {
      RETURN_TRUSTED_TYPE: false
    })});
el.innerHTML = p.createHTML(s);
```

Use a trusted-types policy-name restriction where appropriate and audit policy creation, HTML sanitiser configuration, callers and later transformations. Trusted Types is not automatic sanitisation or proof that only one safe policy exists. Report-only mode observes violations but does not enforce them.
